<div class="meta">Drill 0022 · pairs with lesson 0022</div>
<h1>Drill: Propose, Validate, Commit</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. The model replies with one perfect fact and one with
direction "telepathy". What commits?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Nothing. Validation is
all-or-nothing — a partially-bad proposal yields an empty result, so
half-good output can't half-corrupt the store.</p></div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. The player tells Marge the same secret twice, a game
day apart. How many facts exist and whose timestamp wins?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>One.
<code>factIdFor</code> hashes subject+content, and <code>addFact</code>
keeps the existing record — the FIRST commit's stamp and source survive.
The second commit only (re-)grants knowledge.</p></div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. Why must gossip spread by flipping knowledge bits on
the store instead of NPC→NPC messages?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Any future reader (the
journal tonight, quests later) sees the complete knowledge state by
reading ONE place. A message channel would put truth in transit — the
journal would need to eavesdrop on every conversation instead of querying
the bus.</p></div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Name the three propagation gates and what each
protects.</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Radius (6u): gossip
requires actual proximity — schedules create the encounters. Age (30 game
minutes): a secret doesn't teleport across town the tick after it's told.
Chance (35%, one fact per pair per tick): spread is gradual and traceable
— conservative by instruction, easier to loosen than walk back.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. Journal bonus: why is same-subject SAME-content from
two tellers not flagged?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>That's corroboration —
two people agreeing. A conflict needs the same normalized subject with
DIFFERING content; both sides get flagged so the player sees the
disagreement, not a verdict.</p></div></details></div>
